WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.017
Now we combine several cleaning decisions into one workflow.

00:00:04.017 --> 00:00:12.647
The example removes a confirmed duplicate, converts amount and date safely, standardizes region and channel labels,

00:00:12.647 --> 00:00:16.582
and creates flags for invalid numeric values.

00:00:16.582 --> 00:00:22.840
Read the code from top to bottom and connect each line to a specific data-quality problem.

00:00:23.575 --> 00:00:28.152
Notice that the pipeline does not silently delete every suspicious row.

00:00:28.152 --> 00:00:33.427
Invalid amounts and scores are flagged so they remain visible for review.

00:00:33.427 --> 00:00:39.602
This is a strong pattern for real analytical work: automate what you know is safe,

00:00:39.602 --> 00:00:46.042
preserve uncertainty where investigation is still needed, and keep the transformations readable.

00:00:46.042 --> 00:00:53.177
Run the code and inspect both the cleaned DataFrame and the missing-value summary after conversion.

00:00:53.177 --> 00:00:59.811
A good pipeline should make the data better while also making the cleaning logic easy to understand.
